Day 11 of my DevOps Troubleshooting Series

🔥 Scenario: Application is running, but users are getting 503 Service Unavailable

Imagine you're the DevOps engineer on call. The EC2 instances are running, but users suddenly start seeing:

503 Service Unavailable

What would you check?

Here’s my troubleshooting approach:

🔹 1. Check Load Balancer Target Health
First, verify whether the targets behind the Load Balancer are healthy.

🔹 2. Check Health Check Configuration
Verify:
• Health check path
• Port
• Protocol
• Expected HTTP status code

🔹 3. Check Application Status
Make sure the application process is actually running on the backend server.

🔹 4. Check Application Logs
Look for:
• Application crashes
• Database connection issues
• Configuration errors
• Port-related issues

🔹 5. Check Server Resources
Verify CPU, memory, and disk usage.

Commands I commonly check:

top
free -m
df -h

🔹 6. Check Security Groups & Connectivity
Make sure the Load Balancer can reach the backend application on the required port.

🔹 7. Check Recent Changes
Look for recent:
• Deployments
• Configuration changes
• Security Group changes
• Infrastructure changes

If the issue started immediately after a deployment, rollback can be considered.

💡 Key Learning:

A 503 error doesn't always mean that the server is completely down.

The correct approach is to troubleshoot layer by layer:

